<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
	Static analysis over src/, the JSON-RPC surface handed an MCP client,
	reaching MetaModel and UserRights directly (doc/security-summary.md §4).
	CodeQL has no PHP support and phpcs (phpcs.xml.dist) is a coding-standard
	linter, not a security scanner - but what runs here is Psalm's plain
	type-correctness analysis, not a security check either: the taint
	analysis flag that would actually look for a vulnerability class
	(tainted input reaching MetaModel/UserRights) is off, for the reason
	below. Until it can be turned on, nothing in this pipeline scans src/
	for a vulnerability class.

	Psalm's taint analysis flag is deliberately not run here: it reproducibly
	stalled partway through in every environment this was tried in,
	independent of Psalm's version, of memory limits, and of pcntl
	availability, so no baseline could be generated for it and shipping it
	would either hang the job or pass vacuously having analysed nothing.
	Plain analysis runs reliably and is what psalm-baseline.xml was built
	from.

	This job runs with no iTop checkout, so every core symbol this module
	calls (MetaModel, DBObject, UserRights, cmdbAbstractObject, and the rest)
	is genuinely undefined to Psalm here, not merely unresolved. The
	UndefinedClass family below is suppressed for exactly that reason and no
	other: "undefined" is not a finding when the definition was never going
	to be in this tree. Nothing under vendor/ is scanned either, per
	itop-extension-guide.md §7.1.

	findUnusedCode is off for the same class of reason: this scan sees only
	src/, never iTop calling an extension point by convention
	(RegisterServiceProvider) or through InterfaceDiscovery, and never a tool
	pack calling the @api surface from outside this repository. Every one of
	those would read as unused code that plainly is not.

	findUnusedBaselineEntry is off too: a baseline entry matches by file,
	issue type and the exact reported code snippet, so an unrelated
	reformat a line above is enough to make one stop matching. Turning this
	on would fail the build on that drift rather than on anything new.
	Regenerate psalm-baseline.xml by hand (Psalm's set-baseline flag) when
	triaging the entries it already holds.
-->
<psalm
	errorLevel="4"
	resolveFromConfigFile="true"
	errorBaseline="psalm-baseline.xml"
	findUnusedBaselineEntry="false"
	findUnusedCode="false"
>
	<projectFiles>
		<directory name="src" />
	</projectFiles>

	<issueHandlers>
		<UndefinedClass errorLevel="suppress" />
		<UndefinedDocblockClass errorLevel="suppress" />
		<UndefinedMethod errorLevel="suppress" />
		<UndefinedInterfaceMethod errorLevel="suppress" />
		<UndefinedPropertyFetch errorLevel="suppress" />
		<UndefinedFunction errorLevel="suppress" />
		<UndefinedConstant errorLevel="suppress" />
		<MissingDependency errorLevel="suppress" />
	</issueHandlers>
</psalm>
